#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502216 :tutorial/content "Some real-world situations involve a quantity changing by the same amount repeatedly.\n\nWhen the same change occurs repeatedly, we can represent the total change using multiplication.\n\nFor example, suppose a submarine begins at sea level and descends $2\\,\\text{m}$ each minute for $4$ minutes.\n\nSince descending represents a decrease in elevation, the change each minute is $-2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nThe total change can be written as repeated subtraction:\n\n![](images/bd/bd6e5218d475c46dd7f0ac578340124954d00fa940c81e32bffa49638b0e79c3.svg)\n\nThis repeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} \\times (-2)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n4 \\times (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ minutes, the submarine's elevation is $-8\\,\\text{m}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e61ac714-2900-508a-9e66-9731f70e9997" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15164 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502215 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we know the total change in a quantity and want to find how much the quantity changes during each equal time period.\n\nIn these situations, we use division.\n\nFor example, suppose a total of $\\[math]24$ is withdrawn from a bank account over $6$ days in equal amounts.\n\nSince money is withdrawn, the total change in the balance is $-\\[/math]24$.\n\nTo find the change each day, we divide the total change by the number of days:\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-24 \\div 6 = -4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the balance changes by $-\\[math]4$ each day." :tutorial/id #uuid "2472ba13-0b69-56f2-a477-ea1b2a6fc7e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15182 :tutorial/title "Solving Problems Using Division of Rational Numbers"}]}
